  "dom_id": "step-q50242",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=2{e}^{3t},\\,y=3{e}^{t+2}-5$ where $t$ is a real number. Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=6{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=3{e}^{t+2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3{e}^{t+2}}{6{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2-2t}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2-2t})}{6{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{2-2t}}{6{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
